# Preflight - investigate-mobaxterm-themes - 2026-09-02

## Verified against reality

The plan's central factual claim holds up. Mobatek's own documentation states that `MobaXterm.ini` lives in `%MyDocuments%\MobaXterm` for the installed edition, beside the executable for the portable edition, and in `%AppData%\MobaXterm` "with some versions" - so both default roots the plan scans are vendor-documented, and the portable-edition-is-extraDirs-only decision is correct. Surveyed real theme files (dracula/mobaxterm `MobaXterm.ini`, catppuccin/mobaxterm `.mxtcolors`, `mbadolato/iTerm2-Color-Schemes/mobaxterm/*.ini`, `DreamScripter/putty_to_moba`) all carry exactly the `[Colors]` layout the plan assumes: `Black`..`White` and `BoldBlack`..`BoldWhite` as `r,g,b` decimal triples, plus `ForegroundColour` / `BackgroundColour` / `CursorColour`, alongside `DefaultColorScheme=N` and sometimes `SyntaxType=N`.

`DefaultColorScheme` indexes a built-in dropdown scheme and coexists with explicit RGB rather than overriding it (Dracula ships `=4`, the theme packs ship `=0`, and both render their palette), so treating a 16-slot `[Colors]` block from a default root as `active` is sound, and the planned "index-only file is not `isUsable`" case is the right and sufficient guard.

A claim circulating in third-party write-ups - that installed MobaXterm prefers the Windows registry over the INI - was checked and does not apply to palettes. `HKCU\Software\Mobatek\MobaXterm` holds credentials and master-password material (the `PasswordsInRegistry` switch), not `[Colors]`. Do not document a registry palette gap; it would be wrong.

Convention checks pass. A new `src/parsers/mobaxterm.ts` matches the existing one-file-per-parse-technique grouping, stays inside the vscode-free core, adds no second VS Code mapping table, and takes `extraDirs` as `systemPatterns.md` requires of every walkable format. No public contract moves: `Palette`, the apply path, and `extensionKind` are untouched. No duplication-in-waiting: nothing in the tree parses INI today, and although `discoverGhostty` walks `extraDirs` with a null extension filter and will therefore read a MobaXterm file placed there, `parseGhostty` needs `palette = N=#hex` / `background =` keys and cannot reach `isUsable` on a `[Colors]` block, so no theme gets listed twice.

TDD encoding passes for both executable units. Units 1 and 2 each place stub-tests, then stub-interface, then write-tests-and-run-red, then write-code-and-run-green, explicitly enough that an implementer cannot code first. Unit 3 is a prose/policy unit (README, STORE, memory-bank narrative) and correctly owes no tests. No scheduled change-detectors: the `termeleon.sources` enum assertion is a cross-file manifest contract (a source discovery runs but settings will not admit is a user-visible break), not a document-content assertion, so it stays.

## Blocking finding - fixable

**The Documents default path ignores OneDrive Known Folder Move.** The plan hardcodes `path.join(USERPROFILE or homedir, 'Documents', 'MobaXterm')`, but the vendor-documented location is `%MyDocuments%\MobaXterm` - and `%MyDocuments%` follows the Known Folder redirect, which points at `%USERPROFILE%\OneDrive\Documents` on any profile signed into OneDrive. That redirect is the Windows default for the exact population this task targets, so for a large and predictable share of installed-edition users, default-path discovery finds nothing while `README.md` and `STORE.md` publish a path that is not theirs. This is the one path edge case the plan's Challenges section does not consider, next to portable-edition, CI-env, and UTF-16 cases it does.

The fix is known and small, which is why this is fixable rather than blocking: probe `%OneDrive%\Documents\MobaXterm` and `%USERPROFILE%\OneDrive\Documents\MobaXterm` alongside the plain `Documents` root (OneDrive sets the `OneDrive` environment variable on Windows), add one discovery case for it, extend `withFixtureHome` to save/delete `OneDrive` as well as `USERPROFILE` and `APPDATA`, and note the redirect in the docs unit.

## Minor findings

**Two production edits sit inside a stub step.** Unit 2 substep 2 adds `mobaxterm` to the `package.json` `termeleon.sources` enum and to `SOURCE_LABELS` while the step is nominally interface-stubbing, so the enum test planned in substep 1 will already be green when substep 3 runs it red. Move both edits to substep 4. The unit's overall test-before-code order is correct, so this is not a TDD failure - only a step that will not go red.

**Byte-triple conversion is planned in the wrong module.** `palette.ts` already owns `fromFloatComponents` as the canonical "colour components to `#rrggbb`" primitive for iTerm2's representation; MobaXterm's `r,g,b` decimals are the same kind of primitive and belong next to it as a sibling helper rather than inline in `parseMobaXterm`. The plan's guard against teaching `normalizeColor` raw triples is right and is unaffected by this - a new sibling function is not a change to `normalizeColor`.

**The American `Color` key alias is speculative.** Every real file surveyed, including Mobatek's own documentation, uses British `Colour`. Accepting `ForegroundColor` etc. is harmless but ships untested surface; either add a case for it or drop the alias.

**The `-i` override is undocumented.** MobaXterm accepts `-i <path>` to relocate `MobaXterm.ini` to any local path, network share, or HTTP URL, so a config can legitimately live somewhere discovery will never look. One line in the docs unit's known-limits pointing at `termeleon.extraDirectories` covers it.

**Optional strengthening of the enum test.** Asserting that every source `discoverThemes` actually runs appears in the `termeleon.sources` enum would lock the contract for all future sources, where `includes('mobaxterm')` only locks this one.

## Advisory - radical innovation

MobaXterm's terminal is PuTTY-derived, and so is its colour representation: PuTTY session exports spell their palette `Colour0`..`Colour21` as the same `r,g,b` decimal triples inside the same `[section] key=value` INI shape. Termeleon supports no PuTTY-family emulator today, and the Windows/WSL audience this task is aimed at is exactly the PuTTY, KiTTY, and SuperPuTTY audience.

So the accretive version of this build is to land two small shared primitives instead of one bespoke parser: a `fromByteComponents(r, g, b)` helper beside `fromFloatComponents` in `palette.ts`, and a minimal `[section] key=value` reader (comments, CRLF, section scoping) that returns section-to-key-to-value and nothing more. `parseMobaXterm` then becomes a thin key-mapping table over those two, and a future `parsePutty` is a second key-mapping table plus a discovery function - no new parsing technique, no new colour conversion, no new mapping table. The cost now is one extra exported helper and one ~25-line module over what the plan already writes; the payoff is that the next Windows format is mapping-only work.

Recorded as advisory only. The plan was not modified to include it.
